achments. Strong if I want to get rid of 2s safely, but wastes the powerful pair of 2s which could block others later.\n3. **Long straight starting from ♠4** – Extremely efficient at clearing 11 cards at once. This would leave me with only ♥4, ♦Q, ♠Q (wait — no, ♠Q is used?), wait — check hand.\n\nWait — Hand:  \n♠4 ♥4 ♣4 ♠5 ♠6 ♦7 ♠8 ♠9 ♣10 ♠J ♠Q ♣Q ♦Q ♥K ♥A ♣A ♥2 ♦2 BlackJoker\n\nSo in the straight ♠4 ♠5 ♠6 ♦7 ♠8 ♠9 ♣10 ♠J ♣Q ♥K ♣A — that uses:\n- ♠4, ♠5, ♠6, ♦7, ♠8, ♠9, ♣10, ♠J, ♣Q, ♥K, ♣A → 11 cards\nRemaining: ♥4, ♠Q, ♦Q, ♥A, ♥2, ♦2, BlackJoker → 7 cards left — still a lot, but many awkward ones.\n\nBut note: ♠Q, ♣Q, ♦Q form a triple — good. And ♥A is single. But two 2s and joker remain — very high endgame power.\n\nAlternatively, playing just the triple 4s clears low cards without overcommitting.\n\nBut here\'s a key insight: **if I play a long straight now, I force both peasants to either follow with a higher straight (unlikely, as they’re rare) or pass. Then I get another turn immediately.** If I can go out in two big plays, that’s ideal.\n\nBut the peasants each have 17 cards — they won\'t go out fast unless they team up well.\n\nAnother danger: if I play a long straight and one peasant bombs part of it (but bombs beat any non-bomb/rocket combo regardless of type? No — correction: **a bomb beats any combination except a higher bomb or rocket**, and can be played on any trick. So yes, a bomb can interrupt my straight even if it\'s not the same type.\n\nThus, playing a long straight risks having it bombed — then I lose momentum and tempo.\n\nBetter to start small, force opponents to reveal strength.\n\nAlso, my hand has a triple of 4s and triple of Qs — I could use one to lead.\n\nBut triple of 4s is lower, so better to play first.\n\nUsing **triple_with_pair: ♠4 ♥4 ♣4 ♥2 ♦2** uses up the powerful pair of 2s — risky, because 2s are second highest pair and critical for defense or ending games.\n\nBetter to preserve the 2s.\n\nSo instead, **triple_with_single** using a low card is better.\n\nFor example: **triple_with_single: ♠4 ♥4 ♣4 ♠5** — uses 4s and a 5, both low cards, helps chain progression.\n\nEven better: **triple_with_single: ♠4 ♥4 ♣4 BlackJoker** — but that wastes the BlackJoker as a throwaway, which is terrible.\n\nNever use jokers as kickers.\n\nSimilarly, using ♥K or ♣A as kicker is wasteful.\n\nSo best kicker is a low card: ♠5, ♠6, etc.\n\nBut look: **triple_with_single: ♠4 ♥4 ♣4 ♠5** is valid — yes.\n\nBut also: **straight: ♠5 ♠6 ♦7 ♠8 ♠9** — shorter, but clean.\n\nBut initiating with a **triple** is stronger strategically than a straight, because:\n- Triples are harder to beat than singles/pairs, but easier than bombs.\n- Peasants may have to spend bombs on triples if they lack matching cards.\n- Starting with low triple pressures them early.\n\nMoreover, I have only one triple of low rank (4s), so it\'s best to play it now before peasants gain control.\n\nThus, best move is **triple: ♠4 ♥4 ♣4**\n\nIt avoids wasting high cards, clears weak low cards, and applies pressure.\n\nReason:  \nPlaying the triple 4s is optimal because it removes three low-value cards that are difficult to offload later, without wasting high-ranking cards like 2s or jokers. It forces the peasants to respond with a higher triplet (likely 5s or above), potentially burning their mid-tier cards, or to pass and give me another turn. Starting with a controlled, unobtrusive play preserves my strong finishers (like 2s, jokers, and potential bombs) while testing opponent holdings.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠4", "♥4", "♣4"]}'}
